`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Pairs, frequencies and the extension ratio",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Formula (1) gives the rate at which each pair turns. Each head has an even number ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"d_r"})," of rotated coordinates. They form ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"d_r/2"})," pairs, indexed ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"j = 0, …, d_r/2 − 1"}),", and each pair rotates as a two-dimensional vector. Pair ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"j"})," turns at ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ω_j = b^(−2j/d_r)"})," radians per token, so at position ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"m"})," its angle is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"m·ω_j"}),". The base ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"b"})," is not itself a frequency. It sets every pair's frequency through this formula. Code calls ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ω_j"})," the inverse frequency because it is the reciprocal of ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"b^(2j/d_r)"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Week 3 showed that the rotary part of the score between a query at position ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"m"})," and a key at position ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"n"})," depends on the two positions only through the signed offset ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Δ = n − m"}),". In causal attention a query sees only keys at or before its own position, so ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"n ≤ m"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Δ ≤ 0"}),'. Because the offsets are zero or negative, "larger" is ambiguous: write a range as a signed interval, such as ',e.jsx(t.code,{children:"−(L_0 − 1) ≤ Δ ≤ 0"})," within training, or bound ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"|Δ|"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The extension ratio ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ = L_t / L_0 ≥ 1"})," divides the target length by the trained length. Serving an 8K-trained model at 32K means ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ = 4"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Write ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"D_h"})," for the head dimension. With full rotary, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"d_r = D_h"})," and every coordinate of the head rotates. With partial rotary, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"D_h − d_r"})," coordinates stay unrotated. An attention logit sums over the coordinates of the head, so with partial rotary it splits into a part from the rotated coordinates and a part from the unrotated ones."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The frequency falls as ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"j"})," grows. Low-index pairs rotate quickly: their wavelength is short, and the same offset changes their angle a lot. Within the training length they complete many turns. High-index pairs rotate slowly: their wavelength is long, the same offset changes their angle little, and they complete few turns. Every method in this chapter changes how these bands behave when ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"L_t > L_0"}),"."]})]})]})]}),`
