`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Rescaling positions rescales every frequency",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Position Interpolation (PI) makes a long input look like a short one ",e.jsx(n,{id:"chen2023",children:"Chen et al., 2023"}),". It divides every position by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ"}),", so position ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"m"})," becomes ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"m/ρ"})," and every offset ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Δ"})," becomes ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Δ/ρ"})," (Formula 2). A 32K input then spans the same range of positions that an 8K input spanned in training."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Under PI the query is at ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"m/ρ"})," and the key at ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"n/ρ"}),", so pair ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"j"})," sees the signed offset ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"(n − m)/ρ = Δ/ρ"}),", and its relative angle is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Δ·ω_j/ρ"})," instead of ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Δ·ω_j"}),". The angle at a single position shows the same thing: ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"(m/ρ)·ω_j = m·(ω_j/ρ)"}),". Rescaling the positions is therefore the same as rescaling the frequencies, and PI can be written as ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ω_j ↦ ω_j/ρ"}),", the third map in Formula (2). We call ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ω_j/ρ"})," the PI frequency of pair ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"j"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"adjacent angle"})," of pair ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"j"})," is the angle that one extra token adds to its rotation, which is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ω_j"})," itself. To compare a remapping with the original, divide the new adjacent angle ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ω'_j"})," by the old one. This adjacent-angle ratio ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ω'_j/ω_j"})," is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/ρ"})," for every pair under PI: each pair turns ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ"})," times more slowly per token than it did in training."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Take the illustrative values ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"L_0 = 8,192"}),", ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"L_t = 32,768"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ = 4"}),". The last target index, 32,767, maps to 32,767/4 = 8,191.75. That value lies inside the half-open range ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[0, 8192)"})," that the trained length spans, but just above 8,191, the largest integer index training contained. So the largest target offset maps back into the original range of offsets, up to that fraction. Aligning the integer endpoints exactly would divide by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"(L_t − 1)/(L_0 − 1)"})," instead. We use the nominal ratio ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ = L_t/L_0"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Chen et al. derive a stability bound for interpolation. In their LLaMA setup, they fine-tune each remapped model briefly, then check loss, retrieval, long-document tasks and quality within the original window ",e.jsx(n,{id:"chen2023",children:"Chen et al., 2023"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Dividing every adjacent angle by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ"})," also has a cost. The fast pairs change their angle a lot from one token to the next, and the model uses them for fine distinctions between nearby positions. PI shrinks their adjacent angle by the same factor as the slow pairs', so they lose resolution too. This diagnosis in terms of frequencies comes from the later YaRN analysis, not from PI's original theorem ",e.jsx(n,{id:"peng2024",children:"Peng et al., 2024"}),"."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Static NTK-aware scaling raises the base",accent:3,number:"4",children:[e.jsx(o,{math:"b'=b\\,\\rho^{d_r/(d_r-2)}",label:"(3)"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
