`]}),e.jsx(p,{}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Reading the adjacent-angle ratio",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The widget plots the adjacent-angle ratio of every rotary pair. Each dot is one pair, from the fastest, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"j = 0"}),", on the left to the slowest on the right. Its height is the pair's new adjacent angle divided by its original one, so a height of 1 means the pair turns as it did in training. Select a dot to read its ratio."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Compare the two methods at both ends of the axis and in between, with No extension as the reference. PI draws a flat line at ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/ρ"}),". NTK-aware scaling starts at 1 and falls to ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/ρ"})," at the last dot. A larger ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ"})," lowers PI's whole line and NTK-aware scaling's slow end, while NTK-aware scaling's fast end stays at 1. Changing ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"d_r"})," changes only the number of dots. The NTK-aware ratio ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ^(−2j/(d_r−2))"})," can be written as ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ^(−j/(d_r/2 − 1))"}),". The ratio depends only on how far along the axis the pair is, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"j/(d_r/2 − 1)"}),", so the curve keeps its shape whatever ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"d_r"})," is."]})]})]}),e.jsx(g,{caption:"NTK-aware scaling at ρ = 4: the fastest pair keeps a ratio of 1, and the ratio falls to 0.25 at the slowest pair.",children:e.jsx(p,{initialMethod:"ntk"})})]}),`
