`]})}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Walking the ramp across 32 pairs",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The question applies YaRN's ramp for the Llama family, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"α = 1"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"β = 32"}),", to the illustrative values ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"L_0 = 8K"})," (8,192 tokens), ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"b = 10,000"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"d_r = 64"}),". Which pairs does YaRN keep, and what is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"γ"})," for pair ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"j = 16"}),"?"]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Combine Formula (4) with ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ω_j = b^(−2j/d_r)"})," to get ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"r_j = L_0·ω_j/(2π) = 8,192·10,000^(−j/32)/(2π)"}),". The count falls by the factor ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"10,000^(1/32) ≈ 1.33"})," from each pair to the next. The fastest pair turns about 1,304 times. Pair 12 still turns about 41 times and pair 13 about 31 times, so pairs 0 to 12 have ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"r_j > 32"}),". Their weight is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"γ_j = 1"}),", and they keep their original frequency. At the other end, pair 24 turns about 1.3 times and pair 25 about 0.98 times, so pairs 25 to 31 have ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"r_j < 1"}),". Their weight is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"γ_j = 0"}),", and they are interpolated as under PI. These are the 7 pairs that slide 1 found never complete one turn within 8K tokens: with ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"α = 1"}),", the ramp's lower boundary is one full turn. Pairs 13 to 24 fall between the two boundaries and are blended."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["For ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"j = 16"}),", the frequency is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ω_16 = 10,000^(−1/2) = 0.01"})," rad per token. Its wavelength is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"λ_16 = 2π/0.01 ≈ 628.3"})," tokens per cycle, so ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"r_16 = 8,192/628.3 ≈ 13.04"}),". This count lies between 1 and 32, and the ramp gives ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"γ_16 = (13.04 − 1)/(32 − 1) ≈ 0.388"}),". With ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ = 4"}),", PI's frequency for this pair is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"0.01/4 = 0.0025"}),", and Formula (5) gives ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ω'_16 = (1 − 0.388)·0.0025 + 0.388·0.01 ≈ 0.00541"}),". The blended frequency lies between PI's 0.0025 and the original 0.01."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"YaRN adds attention scaling to the blend",accent:0,number:"9",children:[e.jsx(o,{math:"a(\\rho)=0.1\\ln\\rho+1",label:"(6)"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
