`]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Partial rotary changes what scaling touches",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The question takes ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ = 4"}),", for which ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"a ≈ 1.1386"}),", and a head whose normalized logit has a rotated part ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ℓ_r = 2"})," and an unrotated part ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ℓ_u = 3"}),". What is the new logit if only the rotated coordinates of Q and K are scaled by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"a"}),"?"]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Start from the normalized logit ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ℓ = ℓ_r + ℓ_u"}),". The dot product of a query and a key is a sum over the head's ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"D_h"})," coordinates. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ℓ_r"})," collects the terms of the ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"d_r"})," rotated coordinates, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ℓ_u"})," collects those of the ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"D_h − d_r"})," unrotated ones, and both carry the factor ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/√D_h"}),". Scaling one coordinate of the query and the same coordinate of the key by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"a"})," multiplies their product by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"a²"}),". With full rotary there are no unrotated coordinates, so scaling Q and K by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"a"})," gives ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ℓ' = a²ℓ"}),". With partial rotary, scaling only the rotated coordinates gives ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ℓ' = a²ℓ_r + ℓ_u"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["For ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ρ = 4"}),", ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"a = 0.1 ln 4 + 1 ≈ 1.1386"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"a² ≈ 1.2965"}),". Scaling only the rotated part gives ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1.2965·2 + 3 ≈ 5.593"}),". Scaling the whole logit instead gives ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1.2965·(2 + 3) ≈ 6.482"}),". The values of ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ℓ_r"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ℓ_u"})," are illustrative. The two results come from two implementation choices: scale only the rotated part, or scale the whole logit. Before you reuse a YaRN implementation, check which of the two it scales."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["DeepSeek-V3 is a real model that makes the whole-logit choice. It uses multi-head latent attention (MLA), which Week 4 covers. In MLA, each head's query and key have a 128-coordinate part without RoPE and a 64-coordinate part that carries RoPE, and the key's RoPE part is shared by all heads. Here ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"d_r = 64"})," of ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"D_h = 192"})," coordinates rotate. YaRN's frequency remap acts only on the RoPE part ",e.jsx(n,{id:"deepseekv3-2024",children:"DeepSeek-AI et al., 2024"}),". Yet when the maximum sequence length exceeds the original, the reference code multiplies the softmax scale ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/√D_h"})," by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"a²"}),", so it scales every logit ",e.jsx(n,{id:"deepseekv3-code",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2025"}),". The part without RoPE is scaled too."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"LongRoPE searches the rescale factors",accent:2,number:"11",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
