`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Searching instead of a closed form",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["PI, NTK-aware scaling and YaRN each compute the new frequencies from a closed-form rule. LongRoPE searches for them instead, guided by perplexity ",e.jsx(n,{id:"ding2024",children:"Ding et al., 2024"}),". It searches two non-uniformities. The first is one rescale factor for each rotary pair, so every pair can be slowed by its own amount. The second is a starting-position threshold: positions before it keep their original angles, and later positions get the searched rescaling. We do not derive the search."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The paper extends in stages. For LLaMA2 it first fine-tunes for 400 steps with the rescale factors searched for 128K. It then replaces them with the factors for 256K and fine-tunes for 600 more steps. A second search on the fine-tuned 256K model reaches 2,048K without further fine-tuning. The paper reports two such 2,048K models: this one, whose weights were adapted up to 256K, and one adapted only at 128K. Finally, it searches once more to readjust the rescale factors for short contexts, for example 4K and 8K. The search over both non-uniformities, the staged extension and this short-context readjustment set LongRoPE apart from the closed-form methods."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The schedule separates two lengths: the length used to adapt the weights, at most 256K, and the much larger length reached by remapping, 2,048K. A low perplexity in the search or a nominal maximum length is not evidence that the model uses its context well. Retrieval, tasks that need distant evidence, checks within the original window and serving measurements decide that."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Among Microsoft's Phi models, only the long-context (128K) ones use LongRoPE. The Phi-3 report extends phi-3-mini to 128K with it, and the Phi-3.5 models use it to go from 4K to 128K ",e.jsx(n,{id:"phi3-2024",children:"Abdin et al., 2024"}),". The Phi-4-mini models support 128K through LongRoPE as well ",e.jsx(n,{id:"phi4mini-2025",children:"Microsoft et al., 2025"}),"."]})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(l,{tab:"Training",children:[e.jsxs(i,{title:"Three interventions, three costs",accent:3,number:"12",children:[e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Intervention"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Question it answers"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Main cost"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Inference-only remapping"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Serve longer without training?"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"None up front; must pass the matrix"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Continued long-context training"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Can you afford to adapt the weights?"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Training compute and long data"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Target-length training"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Must weights see the full length?"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Attention, memory, batch and data"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Three interventions answer three questions",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The remappings of the previous tabs change the angles. Reaching a longer context can also involve the weights, and three interventions answer three different questions."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Inference-only remapping asks whether you can serve longer inputs without any training. It changes the positional coordinates with one of the remappings above, such as PI, NTK-aware scaling or YaRN, and leaves the weights as they are. It costs nothing up front, so it is the cheapest intervention to try. It is acceptable only if the model passes the complete evaluation matrix. The matrix is a set of checks across lengths, evidence positions, tasks that need distant evidence, the original window and serving, and you declare its pass threshold before the run. The Evaluation tab sets out the matrix."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Continued long-context training asks whether you can afford to adapt the weights. It trains the checkpoint further on longer sequences. This costs training compute and needs long training data, and in return the model gets a chance to learn to use distant evidence."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Target-length training asks whether the weights must see the full target length during training. In the chosen training stage it exposes them to sequences of the full target length, and it pays what such sequences cost: attention work, memory, a smaller batch and enough long data."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The three names describe what you do to the model. They do not tell you the compute, memory, data or quality cost that a given run ends up with. Compare those outcomes with the same evaluation matrix."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Each intervention fits a different situation. Remapping alone fits when training is unavailable and the model already meets the declared threshold on the complete matrix. Short continued training fits when the weights must adapt and representative long data is available. Target-length training fits when the weights must see the full target length and its compute, memory, batch and data costs are acceptable."})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Real recipes mix training and remapping",accent:4,number:"13",children:[e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Model"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Training for length"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Remapping"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Llama 3 ",e.jsx(n,{id:"llama3-2024",children:"Grattafiori et al., 2024"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Base 500,000 from the start; 405B: six stages, 8K to 128K"}),e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["None in the report; 3.1 weights rescale by parts ",e.jsx(n,{id:"llama3-code",children:"Meta, 2025"})]})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Qwen3 ",e.jsx(n,{id:"qwen3-2025",children:"Yang et al., 2025"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"ABF to 1,000,000; a 32K stage"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"YaRN and Dual Chunk Attention (DCA) at inference"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["OLMo 3 ",e.jsx(n,{id:"olmo3-2025",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"8K to 65K on 50B to 100B tokens"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"YaRN on full-attention layers only"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["DeepSeek-V3 ",e.jsx(n,{id:"deepseekv3-2024",children:"DeepSeek-AI et al., 2024"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Two 1,000-step phases, 4K to 32K to 128K"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"YaRN on the RoPE part only"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Long-context Phi ",e.jsx(n,{id:"phi3-2024",children:"Abdin et al., 2024"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Extended to 128K"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"LongRoPE"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"What each recipe teaches",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The table lists five recipes from model reports. Each row pairs what the model trains on to reach its length with the remapping it uses, if any."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Llama 3 sets the RoPE base to 500,000 from the start of pre-training ",e.jsx(n,{id:"llama3-2024",children:"Grattafiori et al., 2024"}),". The report writes the base as ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"θ"}),". It is the course's ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"b"}),". For the 405B model the report then increases the context from the original 8K to 128K in six stages, over about 800B training tokens, so the weights see the full target length. The report mentions no YaRN. The report's models were released as Llama 3.1, and Meta's reference code for those weights rescales the frequencies by parts. The code uses the rotation count ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"r_j = L_0/λ_j"})," of slide 7, over the original 8,192 tokens. Pairs with fewer than one rotation have their frequency divided by 8. Pairs with more than four rotations keep their frequency, and the pairs between are blended ",e.jsx(n,{id:"llama3-code",children:"Meta, 2025"}),". The factor and both ramp boundaries come from a grid search, as a comment in the code says. The factor is 8, smaller than the extension ratio ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"L_t/L_0 = 16"}),". This is the mechanism of NTK-by-parts, with its own ramp boundaries and without YaRN's attention scaling."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Qwen3 raises its base from 10,000 to 1,000,000 with adjusted base frequency (ABF), the trained high base of slide 4, for a long-context pre-training stage at 32,768 tokens ",e.jsx(n,{id:"qwen3-2025",children:"Yang et al., 2025"}),". So raising the base covers two different things: static NTK-aware scaling, a formula applied without training, and ABF, where you pick a large base and train with it. At inference Qwen3 adds YaRN and Dual Chunk Attention (DCA), a method that splits a long input into chunks for attention. Together they let Qwen3 serve inputs four times longer than its 32K training length ",e.jsx(n,{id:"qwen3-2025",children:"Yang et al., 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["OLMo 3 extends its context from 8,192 to 65,536 tokens in a training stage of 50B tokens for the 7B model and 100B tokens for the 32B model ",e.jsx(n,{id:"olmo3-2025",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),". During that stage it applies YaRN only to its full-attention layers. Three of every four layers use sliding-window attention, in which each token attends only to a fixed window of recent tokens. Those layers never see an offset beyond their window, however long the input, and OLMo 3 leaves their RoPE unchanged ",e.jsx(n,{id:"olmo3-2025",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),". Week 4 covers sliding windows."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["DeepSeek-V3 extends from 4K to 32K and then to 128K in two training phases of 1,000 steps each, with YaRN applied in both ",e.jsx(n,{id:"deepseekv3-2024",children:"DeepSeek-AI et al., 2024"}),". YaRN remaps only the RoPE part of its multi-head latent attention, the 64 rotated coordinates of the split described on slide 10."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The long-context Phi models, among them phi-3-mini-128K, the Phi-3.5 models and Phi-4-mini, reach 128K with LongRoPE, the searched rescaling of slide 11 ",e.jsx(n,{id:"phi3-2024",children:"Abdin et al., 2024"})," ",e.jsx(n,{id:"phi4mini-2025",children:"Microsoft et al., 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The rows also differ in when they remap. Among the three YaRN rows, only Qwen3 applies it at inference alone. OLMo 3 and DeepSeek-V3 apply it during their extension training. Of these five, three use YaRN, one LongRoPE, and one relies on a high base, with by-parts rescaling in its released weights. No row is a default for every model."})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Remapping keeps angles in range; training improves use",accent:5,number:"14",items:"5",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
