`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Remapping and adaptation do different jobs",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A remapping changes only the position indices, or equivalently the frequencies, so that the angles of a long input stay in the range training produced. The model's internal states at the new lengths can still differ from anything it saw in training, and the remapping teaches it nothing about using distant tokens."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Adapting the weights on long data gives the model practice with distant tokens, and in the reports it improved long-context scores. OLMo 3 compared several token budgets for its extension stage. More tokens raised its scores on the long-context benchmark RULER, especially at longer sequence lengths ",e.jsx(n,{id:"olmo3-2025",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),". Position Interpolation, too, comes with adaptation: Chen et al. fine-tune briefly after interpolating ",e.jsx(n,{id:"chen2023",children:"Chen et al., 2023"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"These recipes worked for their own models. If your own measurement disagrees with what they suggest, trust the measurement."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Training on longer sequences also costs more. Longer sequences raise the attention work and the memory pressure of each step. The utilization a run actually reaches depends on the kernels, the parallelism, how sequences are packed and the hardware."})]})]})]}),`
